Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 3200,
      "net_income_cr": 780,
      "eps": 9.9
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 3750,
      "net_income_cr": 950,
      "eps": 12.05
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 4350,
      "net_income_cr": 1130,
      "eps": 14.35
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Loan disbursements will continue to grow at 25-30% CAGR driven by India's renewable energy expansion targets and IREDA's 82% disbursement growth in FY22, translating to sustained interest income growth of ~17-20% per year as the loan book compounds from the \u20b933,256 crore advances base seen at March 2022.",
    "Net Interest Margins and fee income will improve modestly as the loan mix shifts toward short-term loans and new segments (EVs, green hydrogen advisory), while credit costs (impairment charges) normalize from the elevated \u20b9179.90 crore in FY22 as the COVID-restructured book (\u20b92,668.80 lakh) resolves, supporting PAT margins expanding from ~22% in FY22 toward ~26% by FY25."
  ],
  "reasoning": "IREDA is an NBFC; Total Income (Revenue from Operations + Other Income) is used as the top-line revenue: FY22 \u20b92,859.90 + \u20b914.26 = \u20b92,874.16 crore; FY21 \u20b92,654.81 + \u2082.93 = \u20b92,657.74 crore \u2014 a ~8% YoY growth. However, FY22 is anomalous in that sanctions surged 117% and disbursements 82%, which is far above the modest ~7-8% revenue growth seen in FY21-22 (the lag between disbursements hitting the loan book and interest income recognition means FY23-25 revenues will see accelerated catch-up). Additionally, a \u20b91,500 crore equity infusion in FY22 materially expanded the balance sheet, supporting much higher lending capacity. Given the disbursement surge represents leading indicators for interest income (typical 6-12 month lag), the 3-year CAGR of ~8% is not the right basis; instead a mean-reversion recovery toward 17-20% revenue growth is used for FY23, stepping up as the enlarged loan book seasons. PAT growth is projected to outpace revenue growth as impairment charges normalise and operating leverage improves; EPS is computed on approximately 787 crore shares (post the \u20b91,500 crore equity contribution at face value of \u20b910 implying ~150 crore new shares added to an estimated prior base of ~637 crore shares, giving ~787 crore total shares)."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "IREDA",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Loan disbursements will continue to grow at 25-30% CAGR, translating to sustained interest income growth of 17-20% per year as the loan book compounds from the \u20b933,256 crore advances base.",
      "failure_scenario": "A slowdown in government renewable energy project awards, tightening of RBI lending norms for NBFCs, or rising competition from commercial banks and green bond markets could compress IREDA's disbursement pipeline significantly below 25%, meaning the loan book grows at closer to 10-12%, collapsing the assumed revenue acceleration and rendering the \u20b93,200\u2013\u20b94,350 crore revenue trajectory materially overstated.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "COVID-restructured book (\u20b92,668.80 lakh) resolves cleanly and impairment charges normalize from the elevated \u20b9179.90 crore in FY22, enabling PAT margins to expand from ~22% toward ~26% by FY25.",
      "failure_scenario": "Restructured assets slip into NPA as project delays, PPA disputes, or renewable developer cash flow stress materialize \u2014 a plausible outcome given India's history of stressed power sector loans \u2014 causing credit costs to remain elevated or worsen, compressing net margins well below the 26% target and potentially requiring additional provisioning that offsets operating leverage gains.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "EPS is computed on approximately 787 crore shares, derived from an estimated pre-FY22 base of ~637 crore shares plus ~150 crore new shares from the \u20b91,500 crore equity infusion at \u20b910 face value.",
      "failure_scenario": "The share count estimate relies on an inferred pre-FY22 base that is not verified against actual filings; if the true paid-up share capital differs materially, or if additional equity dilution occurs (e.g., an IPO or follow-on offering in FY23\u2013FY25 as IREDA was planning a public listing), EPS would be significantly lower than projected, making the per-share numbers unreliable as a valuation anchor.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 32,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast is intellectually coherent in using disbursement lead indicators to justify a revenue re-rating, but it layers multiple optimistic assumptions simultaneously \u2014 accelerating loan growth, margin normalization, and clean asset quality resolution \u2014 without stress-testing any of them, creating a compounding upside bias that is unlikely to fully materialize. The inferred share count, unverified base-year revenue decomposition, and the absence of any sensitivity analysis or bear-case scenario further undermine confidence in the specific numerical outputs."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
